      "cleanScript": "In Astoria, Oregon, in late November, the rain does not fall so much as it leans sideways off the Columbia River and presses its weight against every windowpane in town. On the third floor of the Halcyon Marine Engineering building, twenty-seven-year-old Caleb Vance sat at a scarred oak drafting table with a lukewarm mug of black coffee beside his elbow and a forty-two-inch blueprint unrolled across the blotter. The ink smelled faintly of ammonia and damp wool. Outside, the Astoria-Megler Bridge vanished into low gray fog, and the foghorn at the river bar groaned every twenty seconds like an old iron hinge. Caleb was a junior structural engineer, three years out of Oregon State, which meant he got the jobs nobody else wanted: checking load tables, verifying rebar schedules, and auditing the final drawings for the Point Adams Municipal Pier. The pier had been closed since August after a winter storm snapped two outer pilings. Every fishing boat, crabber, and charter captain in the county was losing money while it sat behind orange safety fencing. Caleb\u2019s job was simple: sign off on the reinforcement collar specs so the city could pour concrete on Monday morning. Except at 4:15 PM, his red pencil stopped moving. He leaned closer to Sheet S-14, where the timber-to-steel transition collars met the outer mudline. Somebody had used the summer slack-tide velocity to calculate the lateral scour. They had not factored in the December king-tide surge\u2014when the river meets a forty-knot southwest gale and turns the channel into a hydraulic chisel. the river does not negotiate with a budget.\n\nCaleb rolled the sheet under his arm and walked down the hall to Arthur Pendelton\u2019s office. Arthur was sixty-one, wore suspenders over a flannel shirt, and had built half the docks from Coos Bay to Bellingham. He did not look up from his keyboard when Caleb knocked. \"Tell me it\u2019s ready for the stamp, Caleb,\" Arthur said. \"The mayor\u2019s office is breathing down my neck.\" Caleb unrolled Sheet S-14 across Arthur\u2019s desk, weighting the corners with a brass paperweight and a stapler. \"Look at the collar shear value on pile twelve,\" Caleb said, keeping his voice level. \"The calculation assumes four knots of current. Last December, the buoy at Tongue Point recorded seven-point-two.\" Arthur\u2019s chair creaked as he leaned back. He did not even look at the red pencil marks. \"We\u2019re forty thousand over on steel already, kid. If you flag that collar, we have to re-sleeve all six outer pilings, re-submit to the state, and push the pour into January.\" \"If we don\u2019t,\" Caleb said, \"the first king tide will strip the grout right out of the collar.\" Arthur sighed, rubbed his temples, and pointed a thick finger at the door. \"You\u2019re presenting the final brief at the town hall tonight because I have a flight to Seattle. Do not walk into that room and tell sixty angry fishermen that a twenty-seven-year-old with a red pencil wants to cost them another month of crab season. Stick to the approved summary.\"\n\nBy 8:30 PM, Caleb sat in his parked Subaru behind the Astoria Maritime Annex, watching rain sluice down the windshield. Inside the annex, folding chairs were already scraping against the linoleum floor. His stomach felt like it was full of cold gravel. On the passenger seat lay two folders: the slim, approved summary Arthur wanted him to read, and the thick red-marked calculation packet he had printed at 6:00 PM. Every instinct in Caleb\u2019s body told him to play it safe. He was the junior guy. His student loans were still larger than his savings account. If he embarrassed Halcyon Marine in front of the city council, Arthur would bury him in culvert inspections for the next five years. Worse, he knew what the room would sound like. These were men and women who worked fourteen-hour shifts in freezing spray. They did not want a lecture on hydraulic shear from a kid whose boots had never touched a wet deck. Caleb\u2019s hand hovered over the slim folder. Then he thought about the foghorn groaning out on the bar, and he remembered a line his grandfather, a boatbuilder in Port Townsend, used to say whenever a customer tried to rush a hull plank: the water always finds the seam. He grabbed the thick packet, zipped his rain jacket, and walked inside.\n\nThe annex was packed. Steam rose from damp Carhartt jackets, and the air smelled of wet wool and burnt urn coffee. Caleb\u2019s twenty-minute presentation went fine until the moment he clicked to the final slide\u2014the one he had added in the car\u2014showing the revised January timeline. Before the projector fan even cooled, a man in the third row stood up. It was Ray Kowalski, president of the local dockworkers\u2019 union, six-foot-two, with knuckles like walnuts and a voice built to carry over a diesel engine. \"Hold on, son,\" Ray barked, not waiting for the microphone. \"You\u2019re telling us this pier is delayed another six weeks? Halcyon Marine already billed the city twice for site surveys. How much extra does your firm pocket every time you move the goalpost?\" A low murmur rippled through the room. Two people in the back nodded. The city councilman at the front table stared hard at his notepad, refusing to look up. Every nerve in Caleb\u2019s chest screamed at him to defend himself\u2014to pull out the buoy data, to quote the Oregon Structural Specialty Code, to prove he wasn\u2019t a crook. Instead, Caleb took his hands off the podium. He did not raise his voice. He did not point at the screen. He looked directly at Ray and said, \"Mr. Kowalski, can I ask you a question about a boat?\" Ray blinked, caught off guard. \"What about a boat?\" \"Suppose you\u2019re caulking the transom on a thirty-foot cedar skiff,\" Caleb said, his voice so calm the room had to lean forward to hear it. \"And the owner\u2019s standing on the dock, watching the tide come in, yelling at you to launch because he\u2019s got pots to pull. You look down, and you see one brass rivet is half-seated. Just one. Nobody else can see it from the dock.\" Caleb took one slow step away from the podium. \"If you launch that skiff to make him happy today, you\u2019re a hero at noon. And at midnight, three miles past the bar, when that rivet pops and the transom takes on water, you do not argue with a man holding a hammer\u2014you just wish you\u2019d taken the extra hour on the ways.\"\n\nThe fluorescent lights hummed overhead. Outside, the rain tapped steadily against the high windows. Ray Kowalski stood there for three long seconds, his jaw working. The anger hadn\u2019t vanished, but it had nowhere left to land. \"So we\u2019re the skiff,\" Ray muttered, finally sitting back down in his folding chair. \"No, sir,\" Caleb said gently. \"You\u2019re the crew. My job is just to make sure the rivet holds before you leave the dock.\" Nobody clapped. There were no cheers, no dramatic speeches from the councilman. nobody clapped, and that was the victory. Instead, three captains in the front row pulled out their phones to check the January tide tables, and Ray Kowalski stayed after the meeting to ask Caleb which pilings were getting the new steel collars. The next morning, Arthur Pendelton didn\u2019t say a word about the timeline when he walked past Caleb\u2019s drafting table. He just set a fresh mug of black coffee beside the red pencil, tapped Sheet S-14 once with his knuckle, and walked back to his office. Because a quiet answer does not mean a weak spine\u2014and in a room full of noise, the person who refuses to fight is the one everyone finally hears.",
