      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On the morning of November fourteenth, a wall of advection fog rolled off the Strait of Juan de Fuca and swallowed Port Townsend Bay in less than twenty minutes. [BEAT] Visibility dropped from three miles to the length of a mooring line. [LOWER VOICE] Inside the wheelhouse of the harbor tug Caleb Vance, the air smelled of wet wool, diesel exhaust, and burnt coffee from a thermos that had been sitting on the chart table since four a.m.\n\nCaptain Arthur Pendelton stood with both hands resting lightly on the brass spokes of the helm. He was fifty-eight years old, with salt-white stubble along his jaw and a faded canvas jacket that had seen twenty winters on Puget Sound. [BEAT] His job that morning was simple on paper and unforgiving in practice: escort the six-hundred-foot container ship Nordic Star past Marrowstone Point and into the main shipping lane before the ebb tide turned.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Beside him stood Leo Morales, twenty-three years old, three weeks out of maritime academy, and wearing a brand-new foul-weather parka that still crinkled when he moved. Leo's eyes were glued to the green sweep of the radar screen, where sea clutter and fog banks blurred the coastline into a smear of static. [EMPHASIS: The fog did not care who was right], and it did not care that Leo had graduated top of his class in navigation theory. Out here, the water was moving at four knots, and the steel hull behind them weighed forty thousand tons.\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] \"Captain,\" Leo said, his voice tight. \"I'm losing the shore return on the port side. Should I call the pilot? Should we drop speed?\"\n\nPendelton didn't look up from the compass card. \"Keep your eyes on the depth sounder, Leo. Tell me when it reads sixty feet.\"\n\n[SPEED UP] Five minutes later, the VHF radio on Channel 13 crackled to life. It was Captain Henrik Lindqvist, master of the Nordic Star, calling from the bridge of the massive vessel astern. His voice was clipped, loud, and edged with irritation.\n\n\"Caleb Vance, this is Nordic Star. Your stern light just disappeared. What are you doing down there? Are you even holding the channel?\"\n\n[BEAT] Leo flinched as if he'd been slapped. He reached for the mic, his thumb hovering over the transmit button, ready to rattle off their exact GPS coordinates and prove they were on course. [LEAN IN] Pendelton reached out and gently pressed Leo's wrist down.\n\n\"Leave the mic,\" Pendelton said quietly.\n\n\"But he's accusing us of drifting!\" Leo shot back. \"If we don't answer him right now, he's going to think we're lost!\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] \"Nordic Star, Caleb Vance,\" Pendelton said into his own handset, his voice flat and unhurried. \"Holding steady at six knots. Passing Marrowstone shoal in four minutes. Over.\"\n\nHe clicked off the mic. He didn't defend his seamanship. He didn't explain the radar clutter. [EMPHASIS: Answering a panicked question at panicked speed] only teaches the other person that panic works.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Ten minutes later, the fog thickened into a wet, suffocating curtain. You couldn't see the bow of the tug from the wheelhouse window. The depth sounder beeped\u2014sixty-two feet, then fifty-eight, then fifty-five. The ebb tide was pushing them sideways toward the rocky shelf off Marrowstone Point.\n\n[SPEED UP] Leo's breathing sped up. He leaned over the chart table, his finger stabbing at the paper. \"Captain, we're thirty yards off the centerline! Why aren't we turning to starboard? Did you miss the buoy? Are we going to run her aground?\"\n\n[BEAT] The questions came like stones thrown at glass. At the same instant, the VHF erupted again. \"Caleb Vance! My quartermaster says you're sliding toward the shoal! Why are you not correcting? Who is running that boat?\"\n\n[STILLNESS] [LOWER VOICE] For three long seconds, Arthur Pendelton said nothing. His boots didn't shift on the rubber mat. His shoulders didn't rise. Inside his chest, his heart was beating hard against his ribs\u2014he knew the rocks were close, and he knew a forty-thousand-ton ship couldn't stop on a dime. Every instinct in a man's body tells him to bark back, to defend his record, to match the volume in the room.\n\n[PAUSE 2s] Instead, Pendelton reached up and switched off the cabin heater fan. The low hum vanished. Then he slid the starboard wheelhouse window open six inches.\n\nCold, salt-heavy air rushed into the cab. [BEAT] Leo stared at him as if he'd lost his mind. \"Captain\u2014\"\n\n\"Listen,\" Pendelton whispered.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Out in the white nothingness, muffled by the wet air, came a faint, hollow *clang... clang*. [BEAT] A pause of four seconds. Then *clang... clang*.\n\nIt was the Marrowstone bell buoy. Not on the radar. Not on the GPS. Just iron striking iron on the swell.\n\n[LEAN IN] \"He says we're sliding toward the shoal,\" Pendelton said to Leo, his voice barely above a murmur. \"Listen to the bell. Is it getting louder, or softer?\"\n\nLeo froze. He stopped looking at the blinking red numbers. He tilted his head toward the crack in the window. Four seconds passed. *Clang... clang*.\n\n\"It's... it's off the port bow,\" Leo said, his voice dropping an octave. \"It's steady. We're not sliding toward it. We're holding the line.\"\n\n[WARM SMILE] \"That's right,\" Pendelton said. \"The current is pushing our stern, so the radar makes it look like we're drifting. The bell tells us where the water actually is. [EMPHASIS: You do not argue with the bell].\"\n\nHe picked up the VHF mic, waited one full beat, and spoke to the container ship behind them. \"Nordic Star, Caleb Vance. We have the Marrowstone bell off our port bow, bearing two-eight-zero. Channel is clear. Follow my stern light\u2014it'll show through the break in thirty seconds. Over.\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] On the other end of the radio, there was silence. No more barking. No more accusations. Just a quiet, \"Copy, Caleb Vance. Standing by.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Three minutes later, the fog thinned just enough for the green starboard marker to appear like a ghost lantern off their beam. The Nordic Star glided past the shoal with a hundred yards of deep water to spare. By noon, the sun broke through over Admiralty Inlet, turning the water into hammered silver.\n\nLeo stood by the rail as they coiled the heavy hawser on the aft deck. He looked up at the wheelhouse, where Pendelton was already pouring the last of the cold coffee into a tin mug. [BEAT] The young mate realized something that no academy textbook had ever taught him: [EMPHASIS: calm is not the absence of danger]. Calm is the refusal to let someone else's fear set the tempo of your hands.\n\n[PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] Captain Pendelton never raised his voice that morning, and he never once answered an insult with an argument. Instead, [EMPHASIS: he gave them a bearing they could actually steer by]. [STILLNESS]",
