      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] Every morning at six-fifteen, rain or frost, Evelyn Mercer walked down the gravel embankment behind the old Albright Tannery with a pair of rusted Felco pruners in her coat pocket and a five-gallon bucket in her left hand. [PAUSE 1.5s] For thirty-four years, she had taught eleventh-grade botany at Millfield High, explaining to restless teenagers how a single dandelion could crack a concrete sidewalk if given enough time and a millimeter of grit. Now, at sixty-eight, she was trying to prove the same principle on a half-mile stretch of water that the county map still called Tannery Creek, though most locals just called it the Ditch.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Thirty years of chromium runoff and dumped foundry slag had turned the banks into gray, compacted clay where nothing grew except bindweed and broken glass. The water ran the color of weak tea, smelling faintly of sulfur and wet pennies. [BEAT] Yet every morning, Evelyn knelt in the mud with her knee pad, measuring dissolved oxygen with a handheld meter and planting bundles of dormant black willow stakes along the eroding bends. She had no grant, no municipal title, and no crew\u2014just a pension, a library card, and four hundred willow cuttings she had harvested from a healthy watershed two counties over.\n\nBy October of her second year, the first green shoots had begun to hold the silt. [WARM SMILE] Minnows\u2014actual, silver-bellied creek chubs\u2014had returned to the shallow pool below the footbridge. Evelyn kept her progress in a cloth-bound field notebook, logging water temperatures and root depth in neat, slanted pencil. She thought the hardest part of the job was the cold in her knuckles. [LOWER VOICE] She was wrong. The hardest part arrived in an envelope taped to her screen door on a Tuesday afternoon.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] The Millfield Planning Commission had scheduled a public hearing for November fourteenth. A regional logistics firm wanted to culvert the entire half-mile of Tannery Creek\u2014bury it in corrugated steel pipe and pave over the ravine for a forty-trailer staging lot. [PAUSE 1.5s] Evelyn\u2019s restoration permit, which she had filed as a volunteer steward, was listed on the agenda under a single line: \"Unfunded Ecological Variance \u2014 Review for Termination.\"\n\nThat Thursday night, the basement of the Millfield Civic Center smelled of wet wool, burnt coffee, and fluorescent ballasts. [SPEED UP] Eighty-five people packed onto metal folding chairs. In the front row sat Arthur Vance, the logistics firm\u2019s vice president of site development, wearing a charcoal overcoat that cost more than Evelyn\u2019s car. Beside him sat two engineers with rolled-up blueprints and a county commissioner who kept checking his watch.\n\nWhen Evelyn\u2019s item was called, she walked to the wooden lectern carrying only her cloth notebook and a jar of creek water wrapped in a dish towel. [LEAN IN] Before she could even adjust the microphone, Arthur Vance stood up, not waiting for the chair\u2019s recognition, and turned toward the crowd.\n\n\"With all due respect to Mrs. Mercer\u2019s hobby,\" Vance said, his voice booming off the cinderblock walls, \"this town needs forty-two full-time driving jobs, not a mud puddle with twigs stuck in it. [EMPHASIS: He was waiting for her to flinch.] Let\u2019s be honest with the taxpayers here. You\u2019ve been out there for two years, and the creek still looks like a drainage ditch. How much longer are we supposed to pretend that a retired schoolteacher with a pair of garden shears can undo thirty years of heavy industry?\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] A murmur rippled through the room. Two men in the back row nodded. The county commissioner leaned into his microphone and said, \"Mrs. Mercer, you have three minutes to respond to the economic impact concern.\"\n\nEvelyn felt the familiar heat rise behind her ears\u2014the old classroom instinct to correct the premise, to pull out her dissolved-oxygen charts, to point out that Vance\u2019s own company had been fined twice in Indiana for stormwater violations. [SLOW DOWN] She opened her mouth, and then she stopped. She looked at Vance\u2019s face. He wasn\u2019t asking a question. He was setting a trap. If she argued his numbers, she would sound defensive. If she attacked his record, she would look bitter. Either way, the room would remember a fight, not a creek.\n\n[STILLNESS] So she closed her notebook. She reached into her coat pocket, pulled out a single twelve-inch willow stake\u2014gray, knobby, and completely leafless\u2014and laid it on the lectern beside the microphone.\n\n\"Mr. Vance is right,\" Evelyn said quietly. [PAUSE 1.5s] Her voice was so soft that the murmur in the back row died instantly as people leaned forward to hear. \"If you walk down to Tannery Creek tonight, it looks dead. No leaves. No blossoms. Just gray mud and sticks.\"\n\nVance smirked and crossed his arms, glancing at the commissioner as if to say the hearing was already over.\n\n[LEAN IN] \"In fact,\" Evelyn continued, \"if you had walked down there in November of last year, you would have seen the exact same thing. You would have said, 'That woman is wasting her time.' [BEAT] Because in botany, there is a rule that makes terrible television and worse politics: [EMPHASIS: You cannot bully a root system into growing faster.]\"\n\nShe picked up the willow stake, holding it horizontally between her thumb and forefinger so the fluorescent light caught the tiny, raised bumps along the bark.\n\n\"When you push a dormant willow cutting into poisoned clay,\" she said, \"nothing happens above the ground for months. If you judge it by what you can see from a paved road, it looks like failure. [LOWER VOICE] Meanwhile, six inches under the frost line, those little bumps\u2014lenticels, we call them\u2014are drinking in the heavy metals, splitting them apart, and weaving a net that holds the bank together when the spring thaw hits.\"\n\nShe set the stake down and unwrapped the dish towel from the mason jar. Inside, the water was clear enough to read the label on the back of her notebook. Two tiny, darting shadows flickered near the glass.\n\n\"Mr. Vance asked how long it takes to undo thirty years of industry,\" Evelyn said, looking directly at the front row, not with anger, but with the patient, unblinking gaze she had once used on sophomores who claimed they hadn't copied their lab reports. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"It takes longer than a fiscal quarter. And it takes less time than it takes to dig up forty acres of asphalt once the culvert collapses in a hundred-year flood.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] The room was completely silent. Even the radiator in the corner seemed to stop clanking. Vance opened his mouth to reply, then caught the eye of the county commissioner, who was no longer looking at his watch. The commissioner was looking at the two minnows circling inside the mason jar.\n\n\"I\u2019m not here to argue against jobs,\" Evelyn said, her tone warm and matter-of-fact. \"I\u2019m here to remind you that [EMPHASIS: the creek did not ask for an argument.] It only asked for a foothold. [PAUSE 2s] What looks dead in November is often just holding its breath.\"\n\n[WARM SMILE] She picked up her notebook, tucked the willow stake back into her pocket, and sat down in the third row. She didn't wait for applause, and she didn't glare at Arthur Vance. [SLOW DOWN] Three weeks later, the Planning Commission voted four to one to table the culvert permit indefinitely and grant Tannery Creek a five-year watershed protection buffer.\n\n[STILLNESS] The next morning at six-fifteen, Evelyn Mercer walked down the gravel embankment in the freezing rain. She knelt on her foam pad, pushed another gray stick into the clay, and wrote one line in her notebook: *Root depth holding. Water clear.*",
